modules=[]
def lesson(id,title,category,summary,explain,example,lab,question,options,answer,why,tags,sources):
 modules.append(dict(id=id,title=title,category=category,summary=summary,explain=explain,example=example,lab=lab,question=question,options=options,answer=answer,why=why,tags=tags,sources=sources,depth='introductory lesson + original check',estimatedMinutes=20))
lesson('troubleshooting','Troubleshoot like an investigator','IT foundations','Turn a vague symptom into a testable hypothesis and a verified repair.',[
'A symptom is an observation, not a diagnosis. “The internet is down” may mean one application, DNS, a wireless link, a default route, a proxy, or an upstream outage. Establish scope, onset, affected users and the last known good state before changing anything.',
'Collect evidence, propose the most likely causes, and choose a low-risk test that distinguishes them. Change one variable at a time. Preserve logs and configuration so a successful experiment can be explained and a failed one reversed.',
'After the repair, reproduce the original workflow, check adjacent functions and document the cause, action and validation. Escalation is a technical skill: include exact symptoms, timestamps, steps already tried and business impact.'
],'A laptop reaches a service by IP but not by its name. This narrows the investigation toward name resolution, but an HTTPS failure by IP may also be a certificate-name mismatch. Compare a DNS query with a healthy device before changing DNS settings.',
['On your own device, choose a harmless failed URL or disconnected test network.','Write three hypotheses and a non-destructive test for each.','Collect the error and a healthy comparison.','Record one repair hypothesis, rollback plan and validation checklist.'],
'Which is the strongest first step for an intermittent issue?',['Reinstall the operating system','Collect scope, timing and reproducible evidence','Disable all security controls','Replace every network component'],1,'Evidence narrows the cause and avoids destructive guesswork.',['google-it','a-plus','network-plus','ccna','az-104'],['https://www.comptia.org/en-us/certifications/a/'])
lesson('hardware','Hardware, storage and failure domains','IT foundations','Understand the jobs of CPU, RAM, storage, firmware and peripherals.',[
'The CPU executes instructions; RAM holds the active working set; persistent storage retains data after power loss. A machine can be CPU-bound, memory-constrained or waiting on storage. High resource use alone does not identify the cause of latency.',
'Firmware initializes hardware and hands control to a bootloader. UEFI boot configuration, disk partitioning and encryption recovery material affect recovery. Changing boot or storage mode casually can make a working installation unbootable.',
'Safety comes first: power down appropriately, discharge static safely, follow manufacturer service instructions, and never open a power supply. Back up unique data before storage work. RAID improves selected availability properties; it is not a backup.'
],'A slow machine shows sustained paging and little free RAM while CPU use is moderate. Close or measure memory-heavy processes first. An SSD may improve paging speed, but it does not make insufficient RAM disappear.',
['Create an inventory of your own CPU, installed memory and storage.','Identify system versus data volumes without modifying them.','Record the supported backup and recovery procedure.','Explain which measurements would distinguish CPU, memory and storage bottlenecks.'],
'Which statement about RAID is accurate?',['RAID replaces versioned backups','Every RAID level tolerates disk failure','RAID and backup address different failure modes','RAID prevents accidental deletion'],2,'A mirrored deletion or ransomware-encrypted file remains deleted or encrypted. Recovery needs independent backups.',['google-it','a-plus','rhcsa'],['https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/'])
lesson('os-processes','Operating systems, processes and services','Systems','Reason about processes, threads, permissions and service lifecycle.',[
'An operating system schedules work, manages memory, mediates device access and enforces isolation. A process has an address space and security identity; threads share process resources. More threads do not guarantee more throughput when work is serialized or contended.',
'A service is long-lived work managed by the OS. On systemd-based Linux, inspect units with systemctl and logs with journalctl. On Windows, use supported service and event-log tools. Separate configured startup behavior from the service’s current state.',
'Before stopping a process, understand its owner, dependencies and data writes. A graceful stop allows cleanup; forced termination may leave incomplete work. Record the change and confirm recovery after restart.'
],'A web service is enabled but inactive. Enabled describes boot behavior; it does not prove the process is currently running. Inspect status and logs, then determine why it exited.',
['In a disposable Linux VM, inspect systemctl status for a harmless service.','Read its latest journal messages.','Distinguish PID, exit status, unit enabled state and current state.','Do not stop production or unfamiliar services.'],
'A Linux unit is enabled but failed. What does enabled tell you?',['It is healthy','It is configured for activation through its enablement links','It has no dependencies','Its last exit was successful'],1,'Enablement and runtime health are separate states.',['google-it','a-plus','rhcsa','cka'],['https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemctl.html'])
lesson('files-permissions','Files, identities and least privilege','Systems','Use ownership and permissions without granting broad access.',[
'Linux files have an owner, group and mode bits. Read, write and execute mean different things on files and directories: directory execute permits traversal; directory write controls entry creation/removal, subject to additional rules.',
'Permissions should follow the task and identity. Give a service only the paths and operations it needs. ACLs, SELinux or other mandatory controls can further restrict access even when simple mode bits appear permissive.',
'Never fix a permission error by recursively making everything writable. Inspect path traversal, identity, ownership, mount state and policy logs. Secrets and state files must not become world-readable merely to make a test pass.'
],'Mode 640 on a regular file gives the owner read/write, the group read, and others no access. It does not grant execute. A parent directory without traversal permission can still block access.',
['Create files only inside a disposable lab directory.','Use ls -l and stat to record modes and owners.','Apply chmod 640 to a non-secret test file.','Explain why a parent directory can prevent access.'],
'What does mode 750 grant to the group on a regular file?',['Read and execute','Read and write','Write only','All permissions'],0,'7=read/write/execute, 5=read/execute and 0=none.',['google-it','a-plus','security-plus','rhcsa','cka'],['https://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/red_hat_enterprise_linux/10'])
lesson('shell-data','Shell pipelines and safe automation','Systems','Compose small commands, validate input and preserve failures.',[
'A pipeline connects the output of one process to the input of another. Text processing tools are useful for logs and configuration, but quoting, whitespace and filenames can make casual scripts unsafe.',
'Use dry runs and explicit paths for destructive operations. Capture exit status; a pipeline’s final command can succeed while an earlier command failed unless the shell’s error handling is deliberately configured.',
'Idempotence means repeating an operation produces the intended state without accumulating unwanted effects. Prefer declarative desired state, validation and rollback to sequences that assume they are run exactly once.'
],'A backup script that prints “done” after a failed copy creates false confidence. Check the copy result and verify expected files or hashes before marking the backup complete.',
['Create a small local CSV of fictitious hosts.','Filter and sort it without modifying the source.','Write a script that validates the input path and exits nonzero for missing input.','Test filenames with spaces and record exit codes.'],
'What makes a configuration operation idempotent?',['It always runs instantly','Repeated runs converge to the same intended state','It never needs privileges','It ignores errors'],1,'Idempotence concerns repeatable state, not speed or permissions.',['google-it','rhcsa','terraform','cka','az-104'],['https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/bash.html'])
lesson('ip-subnets','IPv4 addressing and subnetting','Networking','Calculate network ranges and understand when a router is needed.',[
'An IPv4 address is 32 bits. A CIDR prefix says how many leading bits identify the network. In common multi-access subnets with prefixes through /30, two addresses are conventionally reserved for the network and broadcast; /31 point-to-point and /32 host routes are special cases.',
'Hosts compare the destination against their local subnet. Off-subnet traffic goes to a next-hop router, usually the default gateway. A valid address alone is not enough: the prefix, gateway and route table must agree.',
'Private address space is not globally routed. NAT translates addresses but is not equivalent to a complete security policy. IPv6 uses a different address structure and does not use IPv4 broadcast.'
],'192.168.10.77/26 has a block size of 64 in the final octet. Its network is 192.168.10.64, broadcast .127, and conventional usable hosts .65–.126: 62 addresses.',
['Use the built-in subnet lab for /24, /26 and /30.','Calculate a range manually before revealing the answer.','Explain whether 192.168.10.130 shares a /26 with .77.','Compare a host route and a default route in a sample routing table.'],
'How many conventional usable host addresses are in an IPv4 /27 subnet?',['16','30','32','62'],1,'There are 2^(32−27)=32 addresses; conventional network and broadcast reservations leave 30.',['google-it','a-plus','network-plus','ccna','aws-saa','az-104','cka'],['https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4632'])
lesson('dns-dhcp','DNS, DHCP and name resolution','Networking','Separate naming, addressing and connectivity failures.',[
'DNS maps names to records such as A, AAAA, CNAME, MX and TXT. Recursive resolvers cache answers according to TTL and other rules. Authoritative servers hold a zone’s records; a recursive resolver is not necessarily authoritative.',
'DHCP leases configuration such as an address, prefix and gateway. A host may obtain an address yet fail DNS, or resolve names while a firewall blocks the application. Test these layers separately.',
'When diagnosing, inspect the actual resolver, query type, answer and TTL. Split DNS can deliberately return different answers inside and outside an organization. Do not publish internal records or logs containing sensitive names.'
],'A new public record resolves correctly from an authoritative server but not from a cached resolver. Compare the previous TTL and cached answer before assuming the zone update failed.',
['Query a public example domain with nslookup or dig.','Identify record type, answer and TTL.','Explain a CNAME versus an A record.','Sketch how DHCP and DNS differ in a new-device connection.'],
'Which component is responsible for a zone’s authoritative records?',['The DHCP lease pool','An authoritative DNS server','Any web browser cache','A default gateway by definition'],1,'Recursive caches may serve answers, but authoritative servers publish the zone data.',['google-it','a-plus','network-plus','ccna','az-104','aws-saa'],['https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1034'])
lesson('switch-route','Switching, routing and segmentation','Networking','Follow a frame on a LAN and a packet across networks.',[
'A Layer 2 switch forwards frames using MAC-address learning within a broadcast domain. A router forwards packets using a routing table. VLANs segment Layer 2 domains; communication between them needs Layer 3 forwarding and an explicit policy.',
'Loops in redundant Layer 2 topologies can be disruptive. Spanning-tree protocols coordinate loop avoidance. Link aggregation and routing redundancy solve different problems and must match both endpoints.',
'Use the longest matching route prefix. Administrative preference and metrics choose among comparable routes. Segmentation is a design tool: define allowed flows and validate them rather than assuming a VLAN label alone enforces security.'
],'A host in VLAN 10 cannot reach VLAN 20 without a gateway or Layer 3 interface. Allowing routing does not automatically mean every application flow should be permitted.',
['Draw two VLANs and a router using documentation-only addresses.','Label broadcast domains and gateway addresses.','Write a flow matrix of permitted source/destination/services.','Find the longest match among /0, /16 and /24 routes for one address.'],
'Which matching route is normally selected for 10.2.3.5?',['0.0.0.0/0','10.0.0.0/8','10.2.0.0/16','10.2.3.0/24'],3,'Longest-prefix match selects the most specific applicable route.',['network-plus','ccna','security-plus','aws-saa','az-104'],['https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/solutions/enterprise-networks/what-is-network-switch.html'])
lesson('transport-tls','Ports, transport and TLS','Networking','Connect application behavior to TCP, UDP and encryption.',[
'TCP provides an ordered byte stream with retransmission and congestion control. UDP provides datagrams without those delivery guarantees. Applications may build reliability on UDP; QUIC is an example. “UDP is always faster” ignores application and network conditions.',
'A port identifies a transport endpoint, not a guarantee of service identity. HTTPS usually uses port 443, but software can listen elsewhere. Test the expected protocol rather than only whether a port is reachable.',
'TLS authenticates endpoints using certificates and protects traffic confidentiality/integrity. Validate the hostname, trust chain and validity period. Bypassing certificate warnings removes protection and is not a routine troubleshooting fix.'
],'A TCP connection to port 443 succeeds but HTTPS fails because the certificate name differs from the requested host. Network reachability and application trust are separate tests.',
['Use a browser’s certificate viewer for a trusted public site.','Record hostname, issuer and expiration without changing trust settings.','Draw TCP connection establishment and TLS negotiation as separate layers.','Explain why an open port does not prove application health.'],
'What should you do with an unexpected certificate-name mismatch?',['Disable verification','Investigate endpoint/name configuration and trust','Assume encryption makes identity irrelevant','Change every firewall rule'],1,'TLS identity validation must remain meaningful.',['google-it','a-plus','network-plus','ccna','security-plus','aws-saa','az-104'],['https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9846'])
lesson('wireless','Wireless design and troubleshooting','Networking','Balance coverage, capacity, interference and authentication.',[
'Coverage asks whether a client can hear a signal; capacity asks whether enough airtime is available. More transmit power or more access points can worsen contention without careful channel planning.',
'Wi-Fi bands differ in propagation and spectrum availability. Channel width affects throughput and interference. Evaluate client capabilities, neighboring networks and physical obstacles before treating advertised link rate as application throughput.',
'Use supported strong authentication and encryption, separate guest access, and manage firmware. Enterprise identity integration and personal shared passwords have different operational properties.'
],'A busy room has strong signal but poor throughput. Airtime contention, interference and client density are better hypotheses than weak signal alone.',
['Inspect your own Wi-Fi connection’s band and link rate.','Compare application throughput with link rate without stressing shared networks.','Sketch guest versus internal access.','List two coverage and two capacity measurements.'],
'Strong signal with slow traffic most directly proves which fact?',['There cannot be interference','Signal strength alone does not guarantee capacity','The ISP is definitely broken','A wider channel always fixes it'],1,'Capacity depends on airtime, interference, clients and upstream paths.',['a-plus','network-plus','ccna'],['https://www.cisco.com/site/us/en/learn/topics/networking/what-is-wifi.html'])
lesson('identity','Authentication, authorization and accounting','Security','Separate who you are from what you may do.',[
'Authentication verifies an identity. Authorization determines permitted actions on resources. Accounting/auditing records activity. A successful login does not imply permission to administer every resource.',
'MFA uses different factor categories; two passwords are not two independent factors. Prefer phishing-resistant mechanisms where supported. Recovery procedures are part of the security design, not an afterthought.',
'Role-based and attribute-based policies must be evaluated with scope, conditions and explicit denials. Assign least privilege, review access periodically and remove unused identities. Machine identities require the same lifecycle care.'
],'An authenticated analyst can read a report but cannot change the dataset. This is normal authorization, not a failed login.',
['Create a fictitious access matrix for support, analyst and administrator roles.','Identify the minimum permissions for each workflow.','Use the policy lab to test allow and explicit-deny decisions.','Write an access-review checklist without creating real accounts.'],
'A valid login followed by “access denied” most likely concerns which layer?',['Authentication always failed','Authorization','DNS alone','Storage capacity'],1,'Identity can be valid while the requested operation is not permitted.',['google-it','security-plus','aws-clf','aws-saa','az-900','az-104','terraform','pl-300','rhcsa','cka'],['https://csrc.nist.gov/publications/detail/sp/800-63/4/final'])
lesson('risk','Risk, threats and security controls','Security','Choose controls from impact and likelihood, not product names.',[
'A threat is a potential cause of harm; a vulnerability is a weakness; risk combines likelihood and impact in a context. An asset’s business role determines what failure costs.',
'Controls can be preventive, detective or corrective and may be technical, administrative or physical. Defense in depth uses complementary controls; adding many copies of the same weak control does not guarantee resilience.',
'Risk responses include mitigation, transfer, avoidance and acceptance. Document owners and residual risk. A compliance checklist is useful evidence but not proof that all real threats are controlled.'
],'A public storage bucket with sensitive records combines exposure, weak access control and high impact. First restrict unauthorized access and preserve investigation evidence; buying monitoring alone does not remove the exposure.',
['Build a risk register for a fictional small business.','Score three risks with a stated simple scale.','Choose a control, owner and validation method for each.','Record what remains after the control.'],
'What is residual risk?',['Risk before any controls','Risk remaining after controls','A list of all software versions','An insurance policy'],1,'Controls reduce or change risk; they rarely make it disappear.',['security-plus','aws-clf','aws-saa','az-900','ccna'],['https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework'])
lesson('cryptography','Encryption, hashing and key management','Security','Use the right primitive and protect its keys.',[
'Encryption transforms data so authorized key holders can recover it. Hashing produces a digest and is not designed to be reversed. Digital signatures support integrity and signer verification, not secrecy by themselves.',
'Password storage needs a purpose-built, salted password-hashing algorithm rather than a fast general hash alone. Encryption at rest and in transit address different threats. Neither protects data after an authorized application decrypts it for an attacker controlling that application.',
'Key lifecycle includes generation, storage, access, rotation, revocation and recovery. A strong cipher with exposed keys offers little protection. Use managed key services and proven libraries instead of inventing cryptographic schemes.'
],'A file’s SHA-256 digest can detect accidental changes when compared against a trusted expected digest. A digest delivered by the same compromised source as the file is not independent authenticity evidence.',
['Hash a harmless local text file before and after an edit.','Explain why the digest changes.','Draw where TLS keys and storage keys are used in an application.','Write a key-recovery and rotation checklist without handling real secrets.'],
'Which primitive best describes a one-way integrity digest?',['Symmetric encryption','Hashing','Compression','NAT'],1,'A hash summarizes content; it does not encrypt it for later decryption.',['security-plus','aws-saa','az-104','terraform'],['https://csrc.nist.gov/projects/cryptographic-standards-and-guidelines'])
lesson('incident','Incident response and evidence','Security','Contain harm while preserving a reliable timeline.',[
'Preparation includes roles, communication paths, logging, backups and exercises. Detection and analysis establish whether an event is an incident and how far it extends. Treat timestamps, identities and evidence provenance carefully.',
'Containment limits damage. Eradication removes causes and persistence. Recovery restores trusted operation and monitors for recurrence. These stages can overlap; actions should match business impact and evidence needs.',
'Do not experiment on evidence or accuse people based on one alert. Preserve originals, document handling, restrict access and involve the appropriate team. Lessons learned should produce measurable improvements.'
],'A suspected compromised endpoint may need network isolation while memory and disk evidence are preserved according to the organization’s process. Wiping it immediately may destroy the evidence needed to understand scope.',
['Write a fictional incident timeline from five sample events.','Separate observations from hypotheses.','Draft containment and recovery criteria.','List evidence handling and escalation responsibilities.'],
'Why preserve original evidence?',['To avoid all containment','To support reliable investigation and provenance','To make every alert a confirmed breach','To publish it publicly'],1,'Preservation supports integrity and defensible analysis.',['security-plus','google-it','a-plus','az-104','cka'],['https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/61/r3/final'])
lesson('cloud-models','Cloud service models and responsibility','Cloud','Know what the provider manages and what remains yours.',[
'IaaS exposes infrastructure primitives; PaaS manages more of the runtime platform; SaaS provides a complete application. The boundary varies by service, so inspect its actual responsibility model.',
'Cloud does not eliminate configuration, identity, data classification, backup or cost responsibility. Managed services reduce some operational tasks while introducing service limits and dependencies.',
'Public, private and hybrid deployment describe organizational arrangements, not a universal security ranking. Select architecture from requirements, data location, operations, latency and cost.'
],'Moving from a VM database to a managed database may shift OS patching to the provider. You still control data access, schema, retention choices and many resilience settings.',
['Compare a VM, managed database and hosted email service.','Create a provider/customer responsibility table.','Identify one operational benefit and one trade-off for each.','Do not create paid resources for this exercise.'],
'Which responsibility usually remains with a cloud customer?',['Physical data-center access control','The customer’s data-access configuration','Replacing provider disks','Provider building power supply'],1,'Customers still configure identities and access to their data and resources.',['aws-clf','aws-saa','az-900','az-104','terraform'],['https://aws.amazon.com/compliance/shared-responsibility-model/','https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/security/fundamentals/shared-responsibility'])
lesson('resilience','Availability, backup, RTO and RPO','Cloud','Design recovery from explicit business requirements.',[
'Availability is about service access over time. Redundancy can reduce selected failure risks, but correlated failures, bad deployments and data corruption can affect replicas together.',
'RTO is the target time to restore service after disruption. RPO is the target maximum data-loss interval. A backup schedule that runs daily cannot automatically satisfy a five-minute RPO.',
'Test restores, not just backup creation. Keep independent copies and control access. Recovery architecture should include dependencies such as identity, DNS, secrets and network access.'
],'A service needs a one-hour RTO and a fifteen-minute RPO. Daily snapshots alone are insufficient for the data-loss target; the design needs a more frequent protected recovery mechanism and measured restoration.',
['Define RTO and RPO for a fictional shop.','List its dependencies in recovery order.','Write a restore test using disposable data.','Measure a local file restore and document what it does not prove.'],
'A fifteen-minute RPO primarily limits what?',['Maximum target data-loss interval','CPU utilization','Password length','Number of regions'],0,'RPO concerns how far back recovered data may be relative to the disruption.',['security-plus','aws-saa','az-104','rhcsa','cka','pl-300'],['https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/reliability-pillar/welcome.html'])
lesson('observability','Metrics, logs, traces and SLOs','Operations','Measure user-visible behavior and diagnose causes.',[
'Metrics summarize measurements over time, logs record events and traces connect work across components. All can be incomplete or misleading if timestamps, labels or sampling are wrong.',
'A service-level indicator measures behavior, such as successful request fraction or latency. An SLO sets a target over a window. Alert on actionable symptoms, and use detailed telemetry to find causes.',
'Percentiles reveal tail behavior that averages can hide. Compare before and after under controlled workload and correctness requirements. Never treat a synthetic benchmark as proof of production improvement.'
],'Mean latency falls while p99 rises because a small fraction of requests now wait on a lock. Averages alone conceal the degraded user experience.',
['Create ten fictitious latency samples with one outlier.','Compute mean and compare it with the slowest values.','Define a useful SLI and alert for a small API.','Explain what logs would distinguish timeout from authorization failure.'],
'Why inspect tail latency?',['It always equals the mean','It reveals slow experiences hidden by averages','It replaces correctness tests','It proves a service is secure'],1,'A small fraction of very slow requests can materially affect users.',['aws-saa','az-104','cka','rhcsa','network-plus'],['https://sre.google/sre-book/service-level-objectives/'])
lesson('cost','Cloud cost and lifecycle discipline','Cloud','Estimate, tag, monitor and remove disposable resources.',[
'Cloud cost depends on resource size, duration, requests, storage, transfer and service-specific pricing. A free tier is a conditional allowance, not permission to assume a lab is free.',
'Use budgets and alerts, but understand they may not be hard spending caps. Tag resources by purpose and owner. Prefer local or simulated labs until you deliberately approve a paid environment.',
'Cleanup is part of the lab: inventory resources, delete only disposable items, verify deletion and check for retained disks, snapshots, addresses and logs. Preserve work before removing resources.'
],'Stopping a VM may stop compute charges while disks or reserved addresses continue billing. The resource lifecycle must be checked service by service.',
['Use a provider pricing calculator without deploying.','Estimate a small service for a month.','List costs not included in VM hourly price.','Write a cleanup checklist and a maximum spending decision.'],
'Which assumption is unsafe?',['Storage may continue billing after compute stops','A budget alert always prevents overspending','Costs depend on usage','Unused resources need review'],1,'Alerts and enforced limits are different mechanisms.',['aws-clf','aws-saa','az-900','az-104','terraform'],['https://aws.amazon.com/aws-cost-management/','https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cost-management-billing/'])
lesson('aws-design','AWS architecture decisions','AWS','Choose services from access, resilience and operational requirements.',[
'EC2 offers virtual machines, Lambda runs event-driven functions, and managed container services support other execution models. The best choice depends on duration, scaling, state and operational needs.',
'S3 is object storage, EBS provides block storage for supported compute use, and EFS provides a shared file interface. RDS and DynamoDB serve different relational and key-value/document access patterns.',
'Design IAM, networking, encryption, backup and observability alongside compute. Multi-AZ and multi-Region choices have different cost, latency and failure properties. Read the specific service guarantees instead of generalizing from a name.'
],'An application stores immutable images and serves them globally. Object storage with a content-delivery layer is a plausible design. It is not a replacement for a transactional database that needs relational constraints.',
['Draw a small web application with a managed database and object storage.','Mark public and private paths.','Describe one-zone failure behavior and backup recovery.','Explain why each service matches the access pattern.'],
'Which requirement most directly favors object storage?',['A shared POSIX filesystem for all workloads','Durable retrieval of objects by key','A CPU instruction scheduler','An IAM login session'],1,'Object storage is organized around objects and keys, not arbitrary block-device semantics.',['aws-clf','aws-saa'],['https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-overview/introduction.html'])
lesson('aws-iam-vpc','AWS IAM and VPC boundaries','AWS','Apply least privilege and trace traffic through the network.',[
'IAM evaluates identity and resource policies with other controls such as organization policies, boundaries and session policies. An explicit applicable deny generally overrides an allow. Do not model all AWS permissions as one flat role list.',
'A VPC has subnets, route tables and connectivity components. Security groups are stateful controls on supported resources; network ACLs are stateless subnet-level controls. Routing and authorization must both permit the intended traffic.',
'Prefer temporary credentials and workload roles to embedded long-lived keys. Verify endpoint policies, DNS and service-specific access as part of the complete request path.'
],'A database in a private subnet needs no public inbound path. An application’s security group can be a more precise permitted source than a broad internet CIDR, where the service supports it.',
['Create a paper-only IAM policy for reading one fictitious bucket prefix.','Identify excessive wildcards.','Trace an application-to-database request through routes and controls.','Use the built-in policy simulator as a simplified exercise, not an AWS emulator.'],
'Which AWS control is stateful?',['A network ACL','A security group','Every route table','A DNS A record'],1,'Security groups track permitted connection state; network ACLs require corresponding directional rules.',['aws-saa','aws-clf'],['https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/reference_policies_evaluation-logic.html','https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/what-is-amazon-vpc.html'])
lesson('azure-governance','Azure identity and governance','Azure','Organize scope, policy and role assignments deliberately.',[
'Azure resources belong to resource groups and subscriptions, with management groups above subscriptions for governance. Scope affects inheritance of role assignments and policies.',
'Microsoft Entra ID provides identity capabilities; Azure RBAC authorizes resource operations. Azure Policy evaluates resource compliance and can audit or enforce selected properties. These are related but not interchangeable.',
'Use tags, budgets, locks and policies deliberately. A lock is not a backup or a substitute for access control. Administrative access should be time-bound and least-privileged where supported.'
],'A policy can require a resource tag while RBAC determines who may create the resource. Passing a policy check does not grant permission to deploy.',
['Draw a management-group/subscription/resource-group hierarchy.','Assign fictional Reader and Contributor roles at appropriate scopes.','Define an audit policy requirement.','Explain the distinction between identity, authorization and compliance.'],
'What is Azure Policy primarily used to evaluate?',['Resource compliance with configured rules','Only user passwords','CPU instruction ordering','Every application’s business authorization'],0,'Policy governs resource properties and compliance; RBAC addresses access to resource operations.',['az-900','az-104'],['https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/governance/policy/overview','https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/role-based-access-control/overview'])
lesson('azure-operations','Operate Azure workloads','Azure','Connect compute, storage, networks, monitoring and recovery.',[
'Azure administration includes deploying and managing supported compute, storage accounts, virtual networks, identity and monitoring. A resource’s existence does not establish that its application is healthy.',
'Use managed identities where supported, choose storage redundancy from requirements, and restrict network exposure. NSGs, routes, private endpoints and DNS interact; diagnose the complete path.',
'Plan maintenance, backup and recovery. Azure Monitor can collect metrics and logs, but collection rules, retention, access and cost still need configuration.'
],'A private endpoint exists but the application resolves the public address. Inspect private DNS linkage and resolution before changing firewall rules.',
['Design a VM and storage deployment without creating resources.','List identity, network and monitoring requirements.','Write a private-endpoint DNS troubleshooting sequence.','Define evidence for a successful backup restore.'],
'What is a useful first check when a private endpoint is configured but traffic uses a public address?',['Delete all security rules','Inspect DNS resolution and private-zone linkage','Increase VM size','Disable TLS'],1,'The client must resolve and route to the intended endpoint.',['az-104','az-900'],['https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/private-link/private-endpoint-overview','https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/overview'])
lesson('terraform-workflow','Terraform configuration, plan and state','Infrastructure as code','Understand desired state and review changes before applying.',[
'Terraform configuration declares resources and relationships. Providers implement API interactions. Initialization installs required provider/module dependencies and configures the backend; planning compares configuration, state and observed infrastructure.',
'State records resource bindings and attributes. It can contain sensitive values even if outputs are marked sensitive. Protect access, encryption and locking where supported; never commit real state or credentials to a public repository.',
'Review plans for creates, updates and destroys. Pin appropriate provider versions, commit dependency lock files for providers, and design modules with clear inputs/outputs. A successful plan is not proof that apply will succeed under changing remote conditions.'
],'Changing a resource attribute may force replacement. A review must inspect the plan’s replacement action and data-loss implications rather than assuming every edit is an in-place update.',
['Write a local, no-cloud configuration using a built-in terraform_data resource.','Run fmt, init, validate and plan only if Terraform is available.','Inspect the plan without creating paid resources.','Explain why state and lock files serve different purposes.'],
'Which file may contain sensitive infrastructure attributes?',['Only README.md','Terraform state','Only a license file','A CSS stylesheet by definition'],1,'State can store values returned by providers, including sensitive material.',['terraform','aws-saa','az-104'],['https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/state','https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/cli/run'])
lesson('terraform-lifecycle','Terraform modules, drift and recovery','Infrastructure as code','Manage change without hiding unexpected differences.',[
'A module is a collection of configuration with inputs and outputs. Good module boundaries model useful concepts, not every single resource wrapped without benefit.',
'Drift is a difference between configuration expectations and actual infrastructure. Investigate whether it reflects an emergency repair, a competing controller or an unintended change before forcing reconciliation.',
'Import connects existing resources to configuration/state workflows; it does not magically infer a complete safe design. State operations are consequential. Back up and understand state before moving or removing bindings.'
],'An operator changes a firewall manually during an incident. The next plan shows drift. Determine whether the change is still required and update the intended configuration rather than blindly reverting it.',
['Create two small local modules with explicit outputs.','Write a change-review checklist.','Describe a drift scenario and an ownership policy.','Do not practice state surgery against production.'],
'What should happen before reconciling unexpected drift?',['Blindly apply','Understand the cause and intended state','Delete the state file','Ignore all future plans'],1,'A plan reports differences; humans or policy must determine which state is correct.',['terraform'],['https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/modules','https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/cli/import'])
lesson('linux-admin','Linux boot, packages and system administration','Linux','Operate a system reproducibly and preserve recoverability.',[
'Know the boot chain, service manager, package manager, filesystems and network configuration of the target distribution. Commands and defaults differ across versions; RHCSA preparation must match its current RHEL release.',
'Use package repositories and verified package metadata rather than arbitrary scripts. Inspect dependencies and service changes. Manage users, scheduled tasks, logs and time synchronization as connected operational responsibilities.',
'Practice recovery in a disposable VM with snapshots and tested backups. Storage resizing, boot changes and firewall changes can lock you out; a lab should have an out-of-band recovery path.'
],'A filesystem is full while the disk has unused capacity. You must distinguish partition, physical volume, logical volume and filesystem sizes before choosing a supported expansion sequence.',
['In a disposable RHEL-compatible training environment, inventory storage layers.','Inspect services and logs.','Create a nonprivileged lab user.','Document backup/rollback before any storage exercise.'],
'Why distinguish block-device and filesystem size?',['They are always identical','Expanding one layer may not expand the others','Filesystems cannot be resized','Only RAM affects disk capacity'],1,'Storage stacks have multiple independently managed layers.',['rhcsa','google-it','a-plus'],['https://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/red_hat_enterprise_linux/10'])
lesson('linux-security','SELinux, firewall and service confinement','Linux','Diagnose layered access controls without disabling them.',[
'Linux discretionary permissions are only one layer. SELinux labels and policy can deny operations that mode bits allow. Inspect relevant audit records and contexts before proposing a change.',
'Firewall configuration should expose only required traffic. A listening process, a firewall rule and a route are separate conditions. Persisted and runtime configuration can differ.',
'Fix the intended policy or labeling using supported methods rather than disabling enforcement globally. Validate the application and ensure the change remains correct after restart.'
],'A web server cannot read content with apparently correct Unix permissions. Check the content’s SELinux context and the service policy before granting broad filesystem access.',
['Read SELinux status and contexts in a disposable lab.','Inspect a sample denial and identify source/target types.','List required service ports.','Write a targeted validation and rollback plan.'],
'What is the safest response to a suspected SELinux denial?',['Disable SELinux permanently','Inspect audit evidence and labels, then apply a targeted supported fix','chmod -R 777 /','Run the service as root without review'],1,'Layered controls should be diagnosed and repaired, not removed wholesale.',['rhcsa','security-plus'],['https://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/red_hat_enterprise_linux/10/html/using_selinux/'])
lesson('containers','Containers, images and isolation','Containers','Separate an image, a running container and an orchestrator.',[
'A container image packages filesystem content and metadata. A running container adds process state and runtime configuration. Containers share the host kernel rather than providing a separate kernel like a full VM.',
'Images are layered and should be pinned appropriately. Keep secrets out of image layers, minimize privileges, and scan/update dependencies. Rebuilding an image is not enough if old vulnerable workloads remain running.',
'Orchestration coordinates placement, health and desired state. It does not automatically make an application stateless, secure or highly available. Storage and identity still require explicit design.'
],'Deleting a container may remove its writable layer. Data that must survive needs a suitable persistent storage design and backup strategy.',
['Inspect an official small image’s manifest or Dockerfile.','Identify base image, process and exposed ports.','Design a non-root runtime with read-only paths where possible.','Explain image versus volume persistence.'],
'What do containers normally share with their host?',['The host kernel','Every process’s private memory','All user passwords','A guaranteed backup'],0,'Kernel sharing is a core distinction from full virtual machines.',['cka','rhcsa','aws-saa','az-104'],['https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/containers/'])
lesson('kubernetes-core','Kubernetes desired state and workloads','Kubernetes','Understand control loops, Pods, Deployments and Services.',[
'The API server exposes cluster state. Controllers reconcile actual state toward declared desired state. The scheduler assigns Pods to nodes; node components run workloads. A manifest accepted by the API is not proof of healthy application behavior.',
'Pods are the smallest deployable units and may contain cooperating containers. Deployments manage replicated stateless workloads through ReplicaSets. Services provide a stable access abstraction to selected endpoints.',
'Use readiness, liveness and startup probes carefully. A bad liveness probe can create restart loops. Requests guide scheduling; limits constrain resources with behavior that differs for CPU and memory.'
],'A Deployment has three Pods but only one is ready. The replica count is not the same as three healthy serving instances. Inspect events, probes, logs and resource pressure.',
['Read a small Deployment and Service manifest.','Explain labels and selectors.','In an approved disposable cluster, inspect rollout status and events.','Document a rollback and never target a production context by accident.'],
'Which mechanism continually reconciles desired and actual state?',['A controller loop','A README alone','A DNS TTL','A local browser cache'],0,'Controllers observe state and take actions toward the declared target.',['cka','terraform'],['https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/architecture/','https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/deployment/'])
lesson('kubernetes-operations','Kubernetes networking, storage and troubleshooting','Kubernetes','Trace failures across workload, node and cluster layers.',[
'Pod networking, Service routing and external ingress/gateway behavior are separate layers. NetworkPolicy enforcement depends on a compatible network implementation. A policy manifest alone is not proof of enforcement.',
'PersistentVolumeClaims request storage through cluster storage mechanisms. Access modes, reclaim policy and storage-class behavior affect availability and deletion. Backups need application-consistent data and tested recovery.',
'Troubleshoot from evidence: context/namespace, resource status, events, logs, endpoints, DNS, node health and control-plane signals. Use the least disruptive diagnostic first.'
],'A Service has no endpoints because its selector does not match ready Pods. Changing the external load balancer is unlikely to fix that internal selection error.',
['Inspect a fictitious Service selector and Pod labels.','Find the mismatch.','Compare a PVC’s status, storage class and reclaim policy.','Write a non-destructive cluster troubleshooting checklist.'],
'A Service has no endpoints. What should you check early?',['Pod labels, selectors and readiness','The color of the dashboard','Every cloud account password','Only the container image logo'],0,'Service selection and ready backing Pods are essential to endpoint creation.',['cka'],['https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/','https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/debug/'])
lesson('data-quality','Data cleaning and reproducible transformations','Data analytics','Make data types, nulls, keys and transformation steps explicit.',[
'Data analysis starts with understanding provenance, grain and quality. A row may represent an order, a line item or a daily summary. Mixing grains can double-count measures.',
'Power Query transforms data through recorded steps. Set data types deliberately, handle nulls, remove duplicates only with a justified key, and preserve the source. Folding can push supported work to a source, but not every step folds.',
'Validate row counts, key uniqueness, null rates and reconciled totals before building visuals. A beautiful report with incorrect joins is still wrong.'
],'Joining orders to multiple line items and then summing order totals duplicates the order amount. Model or aggregate at the appropriate grain.',
['Create a small fictitious orders and order-lines dataset.','Identify keys and grain.','Write three quality checks.','Compare a naive join total with a correctly aggregated total.'],
'What should you identify before combining tables?',['Only their colors','Grain and keys','The final chart animation','A password hash'],1,'Grain and key relationships determine whether joins and aggregations are valid.',['pl-300','google-it'],['https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-query/power-query-what-is-power-query'])
lesson('data-model','Star schemas, relationships and DAX context','Data analytics','Build models that produce understandable measures.',[
'A star schema separates fact tables at a defined grain from descriptive dimensions. Relationships propagate filters. Ambiguous paths and unnecessary bidirectional filtering can produce unexpected results.',
'A calculated column is evaluated and stored according to model behavior; a measure is evaluated in query filter context. DAX row context and filter context are different, and CALCULATE can modify filter context.',
'Use a proper date table where required for time analysis. Verify totals with small examples because a total may evaluate a measure in a broader context rather than simply summing visible cells.'
],'Revenue = SUMX(Sales, Sales[Quantity] * Sales[UnitPrice]) evaluates each sales row’s expression then sums it. A simple SUM of unit prices would answer a different question.',
['Sketch Sales fact and Date/Product dimensions.','Define cardinality and filter direction.','Calculate revenue manually for four rows.','Compare a measure under two filters and explain the change.'],
'What mainly determines a measure’s result in a report?',['Only the file name','Its expression and current filter context','The number of dashboard colors','A fixed value stored for every row'],1,'Measures are evaluated in the query’s context.',['pl-300'],['https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/guidance/star-schema','https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/dax-overview'])
lesson('data-present','Accessible reports and secure distribution','Data analytics','Communicate accurately and control who can see what.',[
'Choose visuals from the question: comparisons, trends, distributions and relationships need different encodings. Label units and time windows, avoid misleading axes, and make color meaning accessible.',
'Row-level security filters data for roles; it must be tested with actual role behavior and sharing arrangements. Workspace roles, app audiences and dataset permissions affect access independently.',
'Publishing requires a refresh plan, data-source access, ownership and monitoring. A report that works only on the author’s laptop is not an operational service.'
],'A sales manager should see only their region. Test the security role and the actual sharing route; hiding a page is not a security boundary.',
['Design a one-page report for a fictional manager.','Include keyboard/readability and color checks.','Define a row-level access rule and test cases.','Write refresh failure and ownership procedures.'],
'Which is not a reliable data security control?',['Tested row-level security','Appropriate dataset permissions','Simply hiding a report page','Least-privileged source access'],2,'Hidden UI can still leave underlying data accessible.',['pl-300','security-plus'],['https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/enterprise/service-admin-rls','https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/create-reports/desktop-accessibility-creating-reports'])
lesson('support-people','Support communication and change control','IT foundations','Make technical work useful to the person affected.',[
'Ask clear questions without blaming the user. Translate symptoms into impact, confirm understanding and explain the next step in plain language. Avoid collecting passwords or unnecessary personal data.',
'Good tickets record environment, symptoms, scope, timestamps, evidence, actions and outcome. Distinguish a workaround from a root-cause repair. Knowledge articles should state prerequisites and rollback.',
'Change control scales with risk. A planned change needs an owner, test, approval where required, implementation window, rollback and validation. Emergency work still needs a record.'
],'“Restarted, seems fine” is weaker than a ticket that records the original error, cause, exact repair and three successful repetitions of the affected workflow.',
['Write a support ticket for a fictional printer issue.','Add a plain-language user update.','Draft a low-risk change plan with rollback.','Remove private details before using it in a portfolio.'],
'What distinguishes a verified fix from a hopeful change?',['A longer ticket title','Re-testing the affected workflow and documenting the outcome','Closing the ticket immediately','Changing multiple settings at once'],1,'Verification connects the action to the original problem.',['google-it','a-plus','network-plus','security-plus'],['https://www.coursera.org/professional-certificates/google-it-support'])
lesson('portfolio','Turn study into employable evidence','Career practice','Build a small, honest portfolio of working systems and decisions.',[
'A certification demonstrates a defined assessment outcome, not automatic job readiness. Employers also look for communication, troubleshooting, practical judgment and evidence that you can finish work.',
'Use a portfolio project with requirements, architecture, tests, security/cost choices and a recovery story. Prefer a modest working system to a huge unverified feature list. Explain trade-offs and limitations.',
'Never publish real credentials, customer data or confidential screenshots. Use synthetic data and document the scope of any lab. Keep an evidence journal of failures and what you learned.'
],'A support portfolio can contain a home-lab inventory, three well-written incident tickets and a tested restore. A cloud portfolio can add infrastructure code, a cost estimate and teardown evidence without claiming production experience.',
['Choose one job family and one small project.','Write acceptance criteria before building.','Record tests and a failure/recovery exercise.','Prepare a five-minute explanation of your decisions.'],
'Which portfolio statement is most trustworthy?',['“Enterprise-ready” with no tests','A precise tested scope with known limitations','A copied certificate image','A list of tools never used'],1,'Honest evidence lets an employer assess real capability.',['google-it','a-plus','network-plus','security-plus','ccna','aws-clf','aws-saa','az-900','az-104','terraform','pl-300','rhcsa','cka'],['https://www.coursera.org/professional-certificates/google-it-support'])
lesson('programming','Programming: values, control flow and functions','Coding','Build a precise mental model before memorizing frameworks.',[
'A program transforms inputs into outputs through explicit rules. Distinguish values, variables, types and operations. A string that looks like a number is not necessarily a numeric value; validate and convert inputs at the boundary.',
'Conditions choose paths; loops repeat work; functions package behavior with named inputs and outputs. Make assumptions visible. A function that silently changes shared state is harder to reason about than one with a clear contract.',
'Errors are part of the design. Define what happens for missing input, invalid values and unavailable dependencies. Small examples, tracing and tests reveal misunderstandings faster than a large unexamined program.'
],'A price calculator should reject a negative quantity and distinguish integer item counts from decimal currency. Parsing user input without validation can turn a useful calculation into NaN or an incorrect total.',
['Write a function that computes a total from quantity and unit price.','Specify accepted types and bounds.','Test zero, a normal case, a negative value and malformed input.','Explain each branch without running it.'],
'Where should external input be validated?',['Only after saving it','At a clearly defined boundary before relying on it','Never, if it looks numeric','Only in a visual label'],1,'Boundary validation prevents malformed values from contaminating internal assumptions.',['coding'],['https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/','https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn_web_development/Core/Scripting'])
lesson('algorithms','Data structures and algorithmic trade-offs','Coding','Choose representations from operations and constraints.',[
'Arrays, lists, maps, sets, queues and trees support different operations. A map can provide fast key lookup under its implementation assumptions; a sorted structure supports ordered traversal and range queries.',
'Big-O describes how work grows with input size, not exact runtime on one machine. Constant factors, memory locality, allocation and input distribution also matter. Measure the actual workload without sacrificing correctness.',
'Every optimization needs a baseline, a representative workload and invariants. Test empty input, duplicates, boundaries and adversarial cases. Retain failed measurements rather than selecting only favorable runs.'
],'Repeatedly searching a long list for each incoming ID can create quadratic work. A set of known IDs can reduce typical membership checks, at the cost of memory and hashing behavior.',
['Implement duplicate detection with a nested scan and a set.','Verify identical results on empty, duplicate-heavy and unique inputs.','Measure several sizes after warm-up.','Explain time and memory trade-offs rather than declaring one universally best.'],
'What does O(n) primarily describe?',['An exact number of milliseconds','Growth in work with input size','Guaranteed low memory','A security certification'],1,'Asymptotic complexity is a growth model, not a wall-clock prediction.',['coding'],['https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html'])
lesson('git','Git, review and reproducible changes','Coding','Keep work reviewable and recoverable.',[
'Git records snapshots and their history. A branch is a movable reference, not a copy of the whole project. The working tree, staging area and committed history are distinct states.',
'Before changing shared work, inspect status, recent commits and remote state. Make small coherent commits, run tests and review the diff for accidental files or secrets. Never force-push over unknown work.',
'A good pull request explains the problem, the change, tests and remaining risks. Reproducible builds require pinned dependencies and a documented environment, not merely a successful run on one laptop.'
],'A passing test result from an earlier commit does not automatically validate a later edit. Associate test receipts with the exact source revision.',
['Create a disposable local repository.','Commit a tiny program and test.','Make a branch, edit it and inspect the diff.','Practice reverting your own harmless commit, not deleting history.'],
'What should a test receipt identify?',['Only the project name','The exact source revision and test environment','Only the author avatar','A future planned version'],1,'Evidence is useful only when it can be tied to the code actually tested.',['coding','terraform'],['https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2'])
lesson('web','The web: HTML, CSS, JavaScript and HTTP','Coding','Separate structure, presentation, behavior and transport.',[
'HTML expresses document structure and semantics. CSS controls presentation. JavaScript adds behavior. Native controls, labels and meaningful headings support keyboard users and assistive technology.',
'HTTP requests carry methods, paths, headers and bodies; responses carry status and content. A 200 response is not proof that a business operation was correct. Validate data and handle loading, empty, failed and retry states.',
'Never insert untrusted content as executable HTML. Use text nodes or safe templating, validate URLs, and treat browser storage as untrusted input. Client-side hiding is not authorization.'
],'A search result title supplied by an external API should be rendered as text. Interpolating it directly into innerHTML can create script injection.',
['Build a labeled form with a submit button.','Validate one field and announce errors accessibly.','Render a string containing HTML characters as plain text.','Test keyboard navigation and a narrow viewport.'],
'What is the safer default for displaying untrusted text?',['Assign it to innerHTML','Use textContent or an escaping template','Evaluate it as JavaScript','Put it into an event-handler attribute'],1,'Text rendering prevents the string from being interpreted as markup or code.',['coding','security-plus'],['https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn_web_development','https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/'])
lesson('testing','Testing, debugging and release evidence','Coding','Test behavior, boundaries and failure recovery.',[
'Unit tests isolate small behavior; integration tests exercise component boundaries; end-to-end tests cover real workflows. Each provides different evidence. A mocked browser-like environment is not a real-browser test.',
'Write tests for contracts and failure cases, not only implementation details. Include repeated actions, interrupted operations, invalid input and recovery. A bug regression should fail on the old code and pass on the repair.',
'Release evidence should identify exact code, environment, commands and results. Record failed and unrun checks honestly. A green CI badge is useful only for the commit and scope it actually covered.'
],'A save button that works once may duplicate data on a double-click. Test repeated submission and uncertain responses, not just a successful first request.',
['Write a failing regression for a small bug.','Fix the implementation without weakening the assertion.','Add a boundary and recovery case.','Document what the tests do not cover.'],
'What is a strong regression test?',['One that passes on every implementation','One that fails on the known defect and passes after the fix','One that only checks a screenshot exists','One that skips errors'],1,'A regression test must distinguish the defective behavior from the repaired contract.',['coding','terraform'],['https://docs.python.org/3/library/unittest.html','https://nodejs.org/api/test.html'])
lesson('databases','Databases, transactions and durable data','Coding','Model data and protect correctness under failure.',[
'A relational database organizes data into tables with keys and constraints. Transactions group operations with defined atomicity and isolation properties. The exact guarantees depend on the database and configuration.',
'Indexes improve selected reads while adding write and storage cost. Parameterized queries separate data from query syntax. Never build SQL by concatenating untrusted input.',
'Durability is not established by a successful in-memory test. Exercise restart, backup and restore, migration and failure paths. Preserve last-good data and verify the result before replacing it.'
],'Transferring a balance between two accounts requires both updates to succeed together. Independent updates can leave money duplicated or missing if the second step fails.',
['Create a disposable SQLite database with two related tables.','Add a foreign key and a unique constraint.','Run a transaction that deliberately rolls back.','Verify data after closing and reopening the database.'],
'Why use parameterized SQL?',['It guarantees every query is fast','It keeps input data separate from SQL syntax','It removes the need for permissions','It automatically creates backups'],1,'Parameters reduce injection risk by avoiding unsafe construction of query syntax from input.',['coding','pl-300','aws-saa'],['https://www.sqlite.org/lang_transaction.html','https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html'])
